WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:13.000
There are a few concepts here that probably match what you might be familiar with object-oriented programming language in whatever language that you already know.

00:13.000 --> 00:23.000
But an object can kind of be anything it can be a variable, a property, a class, everything in Python is an object.

00:23.000 --> 00:34.000
And you can think of an object kind of like a generic container. So a list object might contain a sequence of integer objects along with some function objects.

00:34.000 --> 00:50.000
And the list could have a list in it. Everything in Python is an object. So everything that you might be able to do with Python objects, you can do with any of these things that are objects under the hood,

00:51.000 --> 01:00.000
which isn't necessarily the case in other languages. Python really buys heavily into this object-oriented programming model.

01:00.000 --> 01:06.000
But that doesn't really mean that you need to use object-oriented programming in your own code.

01:06.000 --> 01:15.000
Python works perfectly well as a procedural or a scripting language where one command is just executed one after the other like a recipe.

01:15.000 --> 01:21.000
But getting familiar with object-oriented programming will really help you level up as a programmer.

01:21.000 --> 01:34.000
So a little bit about classes. A really important concept for you to be solid on is the difference between a class and an instance of that class.

01:34.000 --> 01:49.000
So for example, the number 42 is an instance of the class int, whereas int itself is the class object.

01:49.000 --> 02:04.000
So you can think of a class as a type of something like a car, and then you can think of an instance like a specific thing such as my car the Subaru, which is a type of car.

02:04.000 --> 02:11.000
Both classes and instances can have variables and methods on them, which is a little bit confusing.

02:12.000 --> 02:20.000
Changing a variable on the class is going to change what you get returned when you call that variable from an instance.

02:20.000 --> 02:26.000
However, when you change things on an instance, it does not affect the class itself.

02:26.000 --> 02:30.000
And we're going to come back to that concept in a second.

02:31.000 --> 02:42.000
I want to cover the self-cure, and you might have seen that peppered into the examples of Python code that you've seen so far.

02:42.000 --> 02:49.000
Self is used inside of classes to refer to a bound instance of that variable or object.

02:49.000 --> 02:58.000
So if we have a class car, and we made a specific instance of that class called my car,

02:58.000 --> 03:11.000
when we call the start method here, to see if that car runs, we pass in this self-cure.

03:11.000 --> 03:25.000
So let's start a new file named chapter underscore 6.py, and you can just go ahead and copy all of this code and paste it in there.

03:25.000 --> 03:28.000
Let's go ahead and run this.

03:28.000 --> 03:42.000
I wish you'd see some output here. It says, my car runs is true, and then my car dot start prints my car is started.

03:42.000 --> 03:53.000
What happens if we set my car dot runs to false?

03:53.000 --> 04:00.000
We'll run this again. We'll see that my car is broken.

04:00.000 --> 04:03.000
It's very sad.

04:03.000 --> 04:21.000
If we make a new instance of this and say my other car is a car, and if I print out my other car, my other car,

04:21.000 --> 04:34.000
I'm going to run this.

04:34.000 --> 04:45.000
We'll see that, oops sorry, did not want to print that out since this already does some printing.

04:45.000 --> 04:48.000
So let's run that again, and we'll see better results.

04:48.000 --> 04:56.000
We'll see that this car is broken, but my other car runs just fine.

04:56.000 --> 05:02.000
So when you change property is on an instance, it only affects that instance.

05:02.000 --> 05:10.000
And in your Python code, self refers to an instance.

05:10.000 --> 05:24.000
So that means that when we called created that instance variable on my car, when we called start on it,

05:24.000 --> 05:31.000
that self keyword told the interpreter to look for an instance variable in my car called runs.

05:31.000 --> 05:37.000
And when we called my other car dot start, it was attached to that other instance.

05:38.000 --> 05:45.000
All instance variables in these classes are going to take self as their first argument.

05:45.000 --> 06:02.000
If we try to call start on the car class, instead of on an instance, let's see what happens.

06:02.000 --> 06:11.000
I'm going to get an error here, and it's going to say that start is missing one required positional argument called self.

06:11.000 --> 06:17.000
And as you write Python code, I promise that you will see this error over and over.

06:17.000 --> 06:19.000
So might as well get familiar with it.

06:19.000 --> 06:31.000
That's because I'm trying to call this method, which is a instance method, it's expecting an instance on the class itself.

06:32.000 --> 06:37.000
And that does not work.

06:37.000 --> 06:42.000
Let's take a look at the opposite scenario.

06:42.000 --> 06:51.000
If I have an instance here called my other car, and I call my car dot start, this should work just fine.

06:51.000 --> 07:11.000
I remove this self keyword here and comment this out, and I'm going to put a stub here called pass, which makes this valid Python code without anything in it.

07:11.000 --> 07:18.000
When I run this, I'm going to see that start takes zero positional arguments, but one was given.

07:18.000 --> 07:24.000
This is also an error that you'll see quite a bit in Python, so make sure that you recognize it.

07:24.000 --> 07:40.000
This means that you are trying to call a method on an instance, which passes self to this method, this class method under the hood, but you forgot to include self here.

07:40.000 --> 07:52.000
So calling start on this instance is passing in self under the hood, if you forget to provide it in the list of arguments, you are going to see this error.

07:52.000 --> 07:55.000
So now that I've added self back in, this should run just fine.

